Hecks.bluebook "DispatchMetaShape", version: "2026.05.20.1" do
  vision "Shape of the resolver-loop glue that today is duplicated across rust/src/runtime/resolve_*_adapters in mod.rs (one copy per dispatcher family : llm, claude_tool, mcp, exec, tts, soon web_tool / sms / compute). The five dispatcher modules (tts_dispatcher, llm_dispatcher, claude_tool_dispatcher, mcp_dispatcher, exec_dispatcher) each pair with a hand-rolled resolver in mod.rs that walks hecksagons, matches by trigger target, builds attrs, fires the dispatcher, and threads the response back through dispatch_cascade. The meta-shape captures that loop ; the dispatcher bodies (ElevenLabs HTTP, MCP stdio, shell spawn) stay as family-specific work. When fully landed, the resolver-loop copies retire and the framework_registry table replaces seed_kernel_hooks's hand-registered entries — i557 part 2 in shape form. Sibling Futamura target to diagnostic_validator_meta_shape (Ruby specializers) and hecksagon_parser_shape (Rust parsers)."
  category "autophagy"

  # ============================================================
  # DISPATCH META SHAPE — i651 design
  # ============================================================
  #
  # The Rust runtime today has FIVE hand-written resolver loops :
  #
  #   rust/src/runtime/mod.rs
  #     resolve_llm_adapters         → llm_dispatcher::call
  #     resolve_claude_tool_adapters → claude_tool_dispatcher::dispatch
  #     resolve_mcp_adapters         → mcp_dispatcher::dispatch
  #     resolve_exec_adapters        → exec_dispatcher::dispatch
  #     resolve_tts_adapters         → tts_dispatcher::dispatch
  #
  # Plus pending : resolve_web_tool_adapters (i569), sms (stub), and a
  # rework of resolve_compute_adapters along the same lines. Each
  # resolver carries 60-180 LoC of near-identical glue that follows
  # the same seven-step shape :
  #
  #   1. Guard empty hecksagons / read debug env var
  #   2. Build `target` = "<aggregate>.<bare_command>"
  #   3. Collect matching adapters (from one of two adapter sources)
  #   4. Snapshot upstream aggregate state into a String map
  #   5. Overlay dispatch attrs (and optionally cross-aggregate state)
  #   6. Fold the adapter instance's declared fields into the attrs
  #   7. Call the dispatcher ; wire the response via cascade or fire-
  #      and-forget per the family's response_field
  #
  # The shape carries that pattern as one row per DispatcherFamily
  # plus N rows per DispatcherFieldMap (adapter-field → attrs key)
  # plus N rows per CascadePayloadField (dispatcher-result-field →
  # cascade attr).
  #
  # ── Relationship to framework_registry.rs ──
  #
  # framework_registry.rs already abstracts the kernel-hook envelope
  # (AdapterFamily, BehaviorKind, KernelHook signature, KernelResult).
  # What it does NOT abstract is the RESOLVER (which adapters to walk,
  # how to match the trigger, how to build attrs, how to cascade the
  # response). That's what this meta-shape adds : the resolver-loop
  # pattern that wraps every KernelHook invocation.
  #
  # When this meta-shape lands + a specializer generates the resolver
  # loops + framework_registry seeds itself from the same fixtures,
  # the dispatcher path becomes :
  #
  #   runtime.dispatch(cmd, attrs)
  #     → command_dispatch (pure domain)
  #     → generic_resolver_loop(framework_registry, cmd_result)
  #         (one loop, walks every registered family,
  #          fires every matched dispatcher)
  #
  # No per-family arm in mod.rs. The seed_kernel_hooks table retires
  # in lockstep with the resolver duplication.
  #
  # ── Two adapter-source sub-patterns ──
  #
  # Today's resolvers split into two source patterns :
  #
  #   typed_list   — `hecksagons.iter().flat_map(|h| h.<family>_adapters.iter())`
  #                  with `effective_trigger() == target` for the
  #                  match. Used by llm, compute, tts.
  #
  #   io_adapters  — `hecksagons.iter().flat_map(|h| h.io_adapters.iter())`
  #                  filtered by `kind == "<family_name>"` and
  #                  matched by the `command` option string ==
  #                  target. Used by claude_tool, exec, mcp.
  #
  # The shape carries `adapter_source` as a discriminator. A future
  # arc may collapse the two into one (forcing io_adapters families
  # to migrate to typed IR, or vice versa) but the shape stays
  # forward-compatible by naming both today.
  #
  # ── Three response wires ──
  #
  # `response_wire` discriminates :
  #
  #   cascade_via_result_into        — the adapter declares
  #                                    `result_into "Agg.Cmd"` and the
  #                                    response cascade dispatches
  #                                    into that target with the
  #                                    payload built from the
  #                                    dispatcher's result fields.
  #                                    Used by claude_tool, mcp, exec.
  #
  #   cascade_via_response_into      — the adapter declares
  #                                    `response_into_target` +
  #                                    `response_into_attr`. The
  #                                    cascade dispatches into the
  #                                    target with a single kwarg
  #                                    (`<attr>: <response_text>`).
  #                                    Used by llm, compute.
  #
  #   fire_and_forget                — no cascade. The dispatcher's
  #                                    side effect IS the response.
  #                                    Used by tts (and any future
  #                                    `response_field :none` family).
  #
  # ── i111-F : single root aggregate with three nested value_objects ──
  #
  # Mirrors hecksagon_parser_shape (LineParser / LineDispatch /
  # ParserHelper) and diagnostic_validator_meta_shape (RubyClass /
  # RubyConstant / RubyMethod). Three catalog rows ; the specializer
  # reads fixtures, not this bluebook.

  aggregate "DispatchMetaShape", "Static schema for the resolver-loop codegen target — DispatcherFamily + DispatcherFieldMap + CascadePayloadField rows live in the sibling fixtures file" do
    # ── Commands ──
    #
    # EmitResolver is the eventual specializer entry point. Given a
    # family_name (matching a DispatcherFamily row) and an output_rs
    # path, the specializer reads the row + its DispatcherFieldMap +
    # CascadePayloadField rows, weaves them into the resolver-loop
    # template (which lands under snippets/ in the retirement arc),
    # and writes one specialized resolver fn at output_rs. Today the
    # command is contract-only — the body is the deferred follow-up
    # work named in the i651 inbox card. Carrying it as a real
    # bluebook command lets the .behaviors smoke dispatch through and
    # proves the meta-shape parses + validates as a domain, not just
    # as a static catalog.
    command "EmitResolver" do
      role "System"
      description "Emit a generated resolver Rust source file for the named DispatcherFamily. Reads the family's DispatcherFieldMap + CascadePayloadField rows from the sibling fixtures and weaves them into the resolver-loop template under snippets/ (deferred per i651 inbox card — the snippets directory is intentionally empty in the design ship). Output is one specialized resolver fn at `output_rs`, byte-stable across runs for a given fixtures snapshot. When fully landed, the five resolve_*_adapters fns in rust/src/runtime/mod.rs all source from this command."
      attribute :family_name, FamilyName
      attribute :output_rs,   OutputRs
      emits "ResolverEmitted"
      then_set :family_name, to: :family_name
      then_set :output_rs,   to: :output_rs
    end

    value_object "FamilyName" do
      description "The DispatcherFamily.name this EmitResolver run targets (e.g. \"llm\", \"claude_tool\", \"tts\"). Must match an existing row in the sibling fixtures file."
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "OutputRs" do
      description "Absolute path where the generated resolver Rust source lands (e.g. rust/src/runtime/resolve_tts_adapters.rs once the retirement arc is in flight)."
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "DispatcherFamily" do
      description "One resolver-loop per adapter family — the contract that lets the generic dispatcher walk hecksagons, match a trigger, build attrs, fire the dispatcher, and wire the response"
      # name: the adapter family name as declared in
      # framework/adapter_families/<name>.hecksagon (e.g. "llm",
      # "claude_tool", "tts", "mcp", "exec"). Also the IR `kind`
      # discriminator for io_adapters-source families.
      attribute :name,                String
      # dispatcher_module: rust/src/runtime/<module>.rs that holds
      # the family-specific dispatch fn (e.g. "tts_dispatcher",
      # "llm_dispatcher", "claude_tool_dispatcher"). The dispatcher
      # body — provider HTTP, MCP stdio, shell spawn — stays here.
      attribute :dispatcher_module,   String
      # dispatch_fn: the public fn the resolver calls. Most families
      # use "dispatch" ; llm + compute use "call". Names the entry
      # point in `dispatcher_module`.
      attribute :dispatch_fn,         String
      # behavior_kind: the framework/behavior_kinds/<name>.hecksagon
      # this resolver implements (e.g. "render_text_to_audio",
      # "invoke_claude_tool", "invoke_mcp_tool"). Used by the
      # generated resolver to look up its KernelHook via
      # framework_registry.lookup_hook (i557 part 2).
      attribute :behavior_kind,       String
      # adapter_source: typed_list | io_adapters
      # typed_list walks hecksagons[*].<family>_adapters (LlmAdapter,
      # ComputeAdapter, TtsAdapter). io_adapters walks
      # hecksagons[*].io_adapters filtered by kind == name.
      attribute :adapter_source,      String
      # response_wire: cascade_via_result_into | cascade_via_response_into | fire_and_forget
      # Picks the response-routing arm of the generated resolver. See
      # the file header for the three wires.
      attribute :response_wire,       String
      # cross_aggregate_attrs: true when the resolver should snapshot
      # EVERY aggregate's latest state into the attrs map (prefixed
      # and bare keys both, last-write-wins bare). Today only :llm
      # does this — for {{Dream_text_fr}} cross-aggregate templates.
      # Defaults false : the dispatched aggregate's state plus
      # dispatch attrs is the standard surface.
      attribute :cross_aggregate_attrs, String, default: "false"
      # cycle_guard: true when the resolver should track fired-adapter
      # names in a recursion exclude-set (gap3 / i220-3 — prevents
      # cascade-of-cascade loops when trigger == response). Today
      # only :llm uses this. Defaults false : most families are
      # cascade-once or fire-and-forget.
      attribute :cycle_guard,         String, default: "false"
      # debug_env_var: the environment variable that toggles the
      # resolver's eprintln debug trace (e.g. "HECKS_DEBUG_TTS",
      # "HECKS_DEBUG_LLM", "HECKS_DEBUG_CLAUDE_TOOL"). Empty disables
      # the debug arm in the generated resolver.
      attribute :debug_env_var,       String, default: ""
      # log_label: the stdout audit-line label the resolver emits
      # post-dispatch (e.g. "claude_tool", "tts", "exec"). Matches
      # `[<label>:<kind>]` or `[<label>]` per the storehouse_log
      # convention (one stdout line per dispatcher invocation).
      attribute :log_label,           String
      # order: emission order — controls the dispatch-after-dispatch
      # sequence in the generated runtime. Today's hand-written
      # order is compute → llm → claude_tool → mcp → exec → tts ;
      # this attr lets the specializer preserve it.
      attribute :order,               Integer
    end

    value_object "DispatcherFieldMap" do
      description "One adapter-field-to-attrs-key mapping per family — the fields the resolver folds out of the matched adapter into the attrs map the dispatcher reads"
      # family: links to DispatcherFamily.name
      attribute :family,         String
      # source_field: the field on the adapter IR (for typed_list
      # families : "voice_id" / "model" / "speed" on TtsAdapter ; for
      # io_adapters families : "tool" / "server" / "args" / "result_into"
      # — keys of the IoAdapter options map).
      attribute :source_field,   String
      # attr_key: the key the resolver inserts into the attrs map
      # the dispatcher reads. Usually equal to source_field ; named
      # explicitly so the shape carries any renaming (none today,
      # but future families may want translation).
      attribute :attr_key,       String
      # required: true if the resolver should reject the dispatch
      # when this field is absent on the matched adapter (e.g.
      # claude_tool's `tool` field — without it the match is
      # incomplete). False = optional, fold when present.
      attribute :required,       String, default: "false"
      # strip_quotes_or_colon: true when the field comes from
      # io_adapters.options (parse_options leaves strings quoted and
      # symbols colon-prefixed ; the resolver strips before
      # folding). False for typed-list fields (already cleaned by
      # the parser).
      attribute :strip_quotes_or_colon, String, default: "false"
      # order: emission order within the family's adapter-field
      # extraction block.
      attribute :order,          Integer
    end

    value_object "CascadePayloadField" do
      description "One dispatcher-result-field-to-cascade-attr mapping per family — the fields the resolver lifts off the dispatcher's KernelResult and lands on the response_into / result_into cascade"
      # family: links to DispatcherFamily.name. Empty / absent for
      # fire_and_forget families (no cascade payload).
      attribute :family,         String
      # source_field: the field on the dispatcher's result struct
      # (e.g. "tool" / "output" / "exit_code" / "ok" off
      # ClaudeToolResult ; "response_text" off LlmResult).
      attribute :source_field,   String
      # cascade_attr: the kwarg the resolver sets on the cascade
      # dispatch. For cascade_via_response_into families, only one
      # row applies — the response_into_attr declared on the
      # adapter (rendered with cascade_attr equal to the literal
      # "<adapter.response_into_attr>" sentinel since the actual
      # kwarg name is runtime-resolved). For cascade_via_result_into
      # families, every row lands as a kwarg.
      attribute :cascade_attr,   String
      # value_kind: str | int | bool — picks the IR Value constructor
      # the resolver uses (Value::Str / Value::Int / Value::Bool).
      # The dispatcher's result fields are typed Rust ; this carries
      # the IR-side coercion.
      attribute :value_kind,     String
      # order: emission order within the family's cascade-payload
      # build block.
      attribute :order,          Integer
    end
  end
end
